    <!-- Common Conversion Errors -->
    <section class="py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-50 mb-3 text-center">Common Conversion Errors</h2>
      <p class="text-center text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Avoid these frequent pitfalls when converting JSON to YAML format.</p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">Invalid JSON Input</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Trailing commas, single quotes, and unquoted keys are common JSON errors. JSON is strict — all keys must be double-quoted, and the last property in an object cannot have a trailing comma.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">String Numbers Lose Quotes</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">In JSON, <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"8080"</code> is a string. In YAML, <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">8080</code> without quotes becomes a number. If you need a string, use <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"8080"</code> in YAML output.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">Indentation Inconsistency</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">YAML requires consistent indentation. The converter uses 2-space indentation by default. Mixing tabs and spaces after manual editing will break the YAML structure.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">Boolean-like Strings</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">JSON strings like <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"yes"</code>, <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"no"</code>, <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"on"</code>, <code class="text-xs px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800">"off"</code> may be interpreted as booleans in YAML 1.1. The converter properly quotes ambiguous values.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100 mb-1">Special Characters in Keys</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">JSON keys with spaces or special characters need quoting in YAML too. The converter automatically adds quotes when keys contain characters that could be misinterpreted by the YAML parser.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

